# Normalise line endings in the repository.
#
# The working copy on Windows is CRLF; every commit so far was made on Linux
# with LF. Without this file that difference is invisible until someone commits
# from Windows, at which point git records a 149-file, 29,923-line change that
# alters nothing. Real changes then become unreviewable, because they are buried
# in whitespace.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores LF in the repository and lets git check out whatever
# the platform wants. Nobody has to configure core.autocrlf on their machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.bundle binary
*.mp4 binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Recorded exchange responses are evidence. They are committed byte-for-byte and
# must never be rewritten by a line-ending or whitespace filter — the trailing
# spaces and leading-space zeroes in them are exactly what the parsers are
# tested against.
backend/tests/fixtures/** -text

# Windows scripts genuinely need CRLF to run.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
